=== Does the fitted curve distinguish between the moves of a single position? ===
Date        2026-08-30
Curve       KING_ATTACK_PENALTY = { 0, 2, 2, 20, 20, 20, 44, 45, 85 }  (king-attack-isotonic-960)
Sample      300 positions drawn from tuning-data/mychess-selfplay-960.epd, restricted to the
            19,163 that carry at least 3 attack units on one side -- positions where the term
            has anything to say at all.  8,896 successor positions, one per legal move.
Quantity    term = (curve[min(unitsWhite,8)] - curve[min(unitsBlack,8)]) * phase / 24,
            evaluated after every legal move; reported as the spread max - min per position.

WHY THIS AND NOT A FREQUENCY COUNT.  A penalty only changes a decision through the COMPARISON
between the moves of one position.  A term that takes the same value after every legal move
cancels in the search regardless of how large it is.  The corpus-level frequency of high unit
counts cannot see that: it counts positions, and the question is about moves within a position.

  spread of the term across the moves of one position (cp)
    median            18.3
    p25 / p75      7.5 / 41.2
    p90               63.8
    maximum          125.0

  effectively constant (spread < 1 cp):   10 of 300   =  3.3 %
  spread <  5 cp:                         45 of 300   = 15.0 %
  spread < 10 cp:                         86 of 300   = 28.7 %
  spread < 20 cp:                        157 of 300   = 52.3 %

CONCLUSION: MAX_UNITS = 8 STAYS.  The clamp was suspected of flattening the term exactly where
danger is highest, which would have argued for extending the table past index 8.  It does not.
Only 0.72 % of king samples exceed 8 units at all (574 of 79,238; highest observed 14), only 33
positions of 39,619 carry 8 or more on BOTH sides, and the term is effectively constant across
moves in 3.3 % of positions.  Extending the table would buy resolution in a corner of the space
that the fit cannot populate anyway -- indices above 8 hold 0.3 % of samples.

WHERE THE SUSPICION CAME FROM, AND WHAT IT ACTUALLY SHOWS.  Lichess SINwv7q4, myChess as white,
13.Qxd7 (-1.47 -> -5.22 by Stockfish 18 at depth 22; 13.f3 holds at -1.39).  Black holds 10 attack units on the white king, white 0,
and applying the curve to all 36 legal moves leaves Qxd7 ranked 1 of 36 -- unchanged from the
static ranking, where Stockfish puts it 15th.  But the term is not constant there; it points the
WRONG WAY.  Qxd7 draws the best term of any move because the queen on d7 bears along the seventh
rank onto g7 and therefore counts as five attack units against the black king.

That is a limit of the quantity rather than of the curve.  "Piece bears on the king zone =>
attacker" does not ask whether the piece is needed elsewhere, and here it is precisely the
defender white is sending away.  No calibration of KING_ATTACK_PENALTY repairs it, and it is
worth knowing before the term is measured: a neutral SPRT will contain cases like this one, and
they are not evidence that the calibration is wrong.
